# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b5), edited. geneva_fairchild's first conversation.

~ start
set geneva_fairchild_met
Narrator: A breathless young woman sorts tied letters with ink-stained fingers, her leather satchel still damp from the mist.
geneva_fairchild [smile]: Good evening! You caught me between runs. Miss Theodora lets me mind the counter when I'm not walking the valley roads.
-> menu

~ menu
* Ask how she came to work the post -> about_self
* Ask about the roads around Wickermoor -> about_village
* Ask about the local folk -> rumour
* [Insight DC 13] Notice why she keeps glancing anxiously at the return bin -> check_ok | check_fail
* Take our leave -> END

~ about_self
geneva_fairchild [smile]: Look at my eyes, lilac as spring heather. The village grandmothers used to cross their fingers and mutter about bad luck.
geneva_fairchild [neutral]: Miss Theodora took me in and called a rare colour a gift, not a curse. I owe her my living.
-> menu

~ about_village
geneva_fairchild [neutral]: Wickermoor is safe inside the lanterns, though Mayor Somerton's folk are stern about the old customs. Past the Founder's Round, the mist plays tricks on the footpaths.
-> menu

~ rumour
geneva_fairchild [weary]: Don't whistle after the clocktower strikes the third hour. The birds in the rafters get terribly restless when folks whistle out of tune.
-> menu

~ check_ok
Narrator: Her thumb worries an opened envelope addressed in her own neat hand, its ink scraped and rewritten.
geneva_fairchild [afraid]: I wrote to my cousin in the low valley last month, and the reply speaks of a quarrel we never had. Even the seal feels cold, like tallow.
-> menu

~ check_fail
Narrator: She sweeps the stray envelopes into her canvas sack and buckles it tight with a brisk nod.
geneva_fairchild [smile]: Just making sure the post marks are dry before the wagon leaves. Miss Theodora hates a smeared address.
-> menu
